  {"id": 146, "topicId": 1, "new_explanation": "New Moore Island (known as South Talpatti in Bangladesh and Purbasha in India) was a small, uninhabited sandbar landform in the Bay of Bengal situated offshore of the Ganges-Brahmaputra delta. Emerging in the aftermath of the severe 1970 Bhola cyclone, the island was located in the mouth of the Hariabhanga River along the contested maritime border between the Satkhira district of Bangladesh and South 24 Parganas of India. Geographically, it lay merely two kilometres off the coast of Bangladesh and slightly farther from the Indian mainland, creating an intense bilateral sovereignty dispute over exclusive economic zones (EEZ) and resource extraction. The island never rose more than two metres above sea level and was permanently submerged by rising sea levels and coastal erosion around 2010, as confirmed by oceanographers from Jadavpur University and satellite imagery. Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are factually accurate, and Reason (R) directly explains why the island was closest to Bangladesh—its location in the estuary delta at the maritime frontier of both nations placed it in close proximity to the low-lying Sundarban coastline.\n\n- Option B is incorrect because Reason (R) directly provides the spatial and geographic rationale for the proximity asserted in A.\n- Option C is incorrect because Reason (R) is factually correct regarding the island's location at the international maritime boundary.\n- Option D is incorrect because Assertion (A) is a verified geographic fact regarding its relative distance to the respective shores.\n\nMEMORY ANCHOR: Moore Island = 'No More' Island (submerged in Hariabhanga mouth between India & Bangladesh)."},
  {"id": 754, "topicId": 4, "new_explanation": "The true physical shape of the Earth is scientifically described as an 'Oblate Spheroid' or simply a 'Geoid' (literally meaning 'earth-shaped'). Statement 1 is incorrect because the Geoid describes the equipotential surface of the Earth's gravity field that approximately coincides with mean sea level, not the Earth's axis of rotation (which is the imaginary line connecting the North and South Poles). Statement 2 is correct because the Earth's continuous axial rotation generates an outward centrifugal force that reaches its maximum at the equator (where rotational velocity is highest, ~1670 km/h) and diminishes to zero at the rotational poles. Over billions of years, this differential centrifugal force caused the Earth's ductile mantle and crust to deform, resulting in an equatorial bulge (equatorial diameter is ~12,756 km) and polar flattening (polar diameter is ~12,714 km, a difference of approximately 42 km).\n\n- Option A (1 only) is incorrect because Statement 1 erroneously equates the geoid with the rotational axis.\n- Option C (Both 1 and 2) is incorrect because Statement 1 is conceptually false.\n- Option D (Neither 1 nor 2) is incorrect because Statement 2 accurately describes polar flattening and equatorial bulging due to centrifugal rotation.\n\nMEMORY ANCHOR: Geoid = 'Earth-shaped' (Bulging Belly at Equator, Flattened Caps at Poles)."},
