  {"id": 1085, "topicId": 5, "new_stem": "Which of the following natural phenomena does NOT trigger a tsunami?", "new_explanation": "A tsunami is a series of planetary-scale water waves characterized by enormous wavelengths caused by the sudden vertical displacement of a massive volume of water. Such displacements are primarily generated by subduction zone earthquakes (megathrust events), explosive submarine volcanic caldera collapses (such as the 1883 Krakatoa or 2022 Hunga Tonga eruptions), or large submarine landslides. In contrast, hurricanes (tropical cyclones) are intense atmospheric low-pressure vortex systems driven by warm sea-surface temperatures and latent heat release. While hurricanes produce devastating 'storm surges' through atmospheric pressure drop and onshore surface wind stress, they do not displace oceanic water columns from the seabed and cannot produce true tsunamis.\n\n- Option B (volcanic eruptions) is incorrect because violent submarine explosions and caldera collapses readily displace ocean water columns and cause devastating tsunamis.\n- Option C (undersea landslides) is incorrect because massive underwater slumps rapidly shift immense water volumes, triggering local and regional tsunamis.\n- Option D (Earthquakes) is incorrect because submarine dip-slip earthquakes along convergent plate boundaries are the leading cause of global tsunamis.\n\nMEMORY ANCHOR: Tsunami = Tectonic Displacement from Ocean Floor; Storm Surge = Wind from Ocean Surface."},
