  {"id": 2267, "topicId": 10, "new_explanation": "Dandeli Wildlife Sanctuary is situated in the Uttara Kannada district of Karnataka, along the banks of the Kali River in the central Western Ghats. Covering over 866 sq km, it was integrated with the contiguous Anshi National Park in 2007 to form the Kali Tiger Reserve (2015). The sanctuary's dense moist deciduous and semi-evergreen forests support Bengal tigers, leopards, Asian elephants, gaur, king cobras, and the rare melanistic leopard (black panther), alongside the Great Indian Hornbill.\n\n- Option B (Jharkhand) is incorrect; Jharkhand is home to Betla National Park and Dalma Wildlife Sanctuary.\n- Option C (Sikkim) is incorrect; Sikkim hosts Khangchendzonga National Park.\n- Option D (Kerala) is incorrect; Kerala hosts Periyar, Wayanad, Parambikulam sanctuaries.\n\nMEMORY ANCHOR: Dandeli on Kali River = Dense Karnataka Western Ghats (Home of the Black Panther)."},
  {"id": 2283, "topicId": 10, "new_explanation": "The Western Ghats (Sahyadris) run north-to-south along India's western peninsular margin. Statement a is correct: Elevation progressively increases from north to south, starting from 900-1000 metres in Maharashtra and rising above 2,000 metres in the Nilgiris and Anaimalai Hills, culminating in Anamudi (2,695 m) in Kerala. Statement b is incorrect: Mahendragiri (1,501 m) is in the Eastern Ghats of Odisha, not Western Ghats. Statement c is correct: Western Ghats average 900-1,600 metres, substantially higher than the Eastern Ghats averaging ~600 metres. Statement d is incorrect because height increases, not decreases, towards the south. Thus statements a and c are correct.\n\n- Option A is incorrect because statements b and d are false.\n- Option B is incorrect because statement b is false.\n- Option D is incorrect because statement d is false.\n\nMEMORY ANCHOR: Western Ghats: Height Increases Southward to Anamudi; Eastern Ghats: Lower and Broken."},
  {"id": 2289, "topicId": 10, "new_explanation": "Evaluating each statement: Statement 1 is correct - Tawang is a prominent high-altitude district in western Arunachal Pradesh, home to the 400-year-old Tawang Monastery, the largest Tibetan Buddhist monastery in India. Statement 2 is incorrect - Kalimpong was carved out of Darjeeling district in February 2017 to become the 21st administrative district of West Bengal, NOT Sikkim. Statement 3 is correct - Alipurduar and Jalpaiguri are both northern districts in the Dooars-Terai region of West Bengal. The question asks for the INCORRECT statement, so Statement 2 is the answer.\n\n- Option A is incorrect because Statement 1 is factually accurate.\n- Option C is incorrect because Statement 3 is factually accurate.\n- Option D is incorrect because Statement 3 is factually accurate.\n\nMEMORY ANCHOR: Kalimpong and Darjeeling = West Bengal Hill Districts (NOT Sikkim)."},
